From ae4cafc03fdc8ad719d5d528cbbe2a61ce09f44d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nigel Breslaw <nigel.breslaw@slint.dev>
Date: Thu, 17 Sep 2026 08:36:37 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 1/1] exfat: limit directory-count read-ahead to one page

Directory counts often stop at an unused entry in the first page. Avoid
reading up to 128 KiB for this metadata-only scan by allowing the
internal directory-entry reader to accept a read-ahead limit. Use
PAGE_SIZE only in exfat_count_dir_entries(). The public
exfat_get_dentry() interface, its 128 KiB limit, and the existing block
plugging and sync semantics are preserved.

WHY IT MATTERS HERE
exfat_count_dir_entries() walks a directory only to count sub-directory
entries and returns the moment it meets a TYPE_UNUSED entry
(linux-6.18.52 fs/exfat/dir.c:1242-1284) -- usually inside the FIRST
page. Every page-aligned entry on that walk goes through
exfat_get_dentry(), which triggers exfat_dir_readahead() with the
128 KiB window sized for a real directory read:

    linux-6.18.52 fs/exfat/dir.c:658-666
        #define EXFAT_MAX_RA_SIZE     (128*1024)
        static int exfat_dir_readahead(struct super_block *sb, sector_t sec)
        {
                ...
                unsigned int max_ra_count = EXFAT_MAX_RA_SIZE >> sb->s_blocksize_bits;
                unsigned int page_ra_count = PAGE_SIZE >> sb->s_blocksize_bits;
                unsigned int adj_ra_count = max(sbi->sect_per_clus, page_ra_count);
                unsigned int ra_count = min(adj_ra_count, max_ra_count);

On a cold card that is up to 128 KiB of physical reads per counted
directory, for metadata nobody reads. The MiSTer workload is exactly
the pathological one -- `update.sh`/Downloader and file-browser catalog
builds count thousands of directories in a row. PR #106 measures the
shipped Update All installed-files pass at 77.677 s / 181.2 MB of SD
reads on stock 6.18 against 26.817 s / 39.5 MB with this patch (5.15
was 39.006 s / 41.5 MB), and a full third-party catalog build at
305.545 s / 1436.9 MB against 65.866 s / 72.7 MB.

