Upstream:     ABSENT, and not attributable to any mainline SHA -- the
              function it parameterises does not exist upstream. `grep -n
              exfat_dir_readahead fs/exfat/dir.c` returns nothing (exit 1)
              against the pinned linux-7.2.6 tree: 7.x replaced the whole
              path with exfat_blk_readahead() in fs/exfat/fatent.c:159-185,
              called inline from exfat_get_dentry() (7.2.6 dir.c:645-658).
              This is the same situation 0050 documents, and the same
              conclusion: fork code, credited to its author, NOT a
              0047-style mainline backport.
Depends on:   0050-exfat-dir-readahead-plug.patch. Same function, no hunk
              overlap: 0050 adds `#include <linux/blkdev.h>` and wraps the
              sb_breadahead() loop inside `if (!bh || !buffer_uptodate(bh))`
              in blk_start_plug()/blk_finish_plug(); this patch changes the
              signature, the max_ra_count initialiser, and the callers.
              MEASURED identity, not asserted: applying 0050 then this
              patch to a pristine linux-6.18.52 fs/exfat/dir.c yields a
              region from `#define EXFAT_MAX_RA_SIZE` to
              `enum exfat_validate_dentry_mode` that diffs EMPTY against
              `git show ae4cafc03:fs/exfat/dir.c`. Numbered after 0050
              accordingly.
0031 order:   0031-exfat-samsung-symlinks.patch also touches
              fs/exfat/dir.c, at `@@ -268,8 +268,10 @@ get_new:` and
              `@@ -396,6 +398,10 @@ static void exfat_set_entry_type` --
              both far above exfat_dir_readahead(), so the only effect is
              a line offset on this patch's hunks.
6.18-ONLY:    Scoped to the 6.18 series, exactly like 0050 and for the
              same reason: it is deliberately absent from
              board/mister/de10nano/linux-patches-beta/series and from
              board/mister/de25nano/linux-patches/. MEASURED against the
              pinned linux-7.2.6 tree: `patch -p1 -F0 --dry-run` reports
              "Hunk #1 FAILED at 656. Hunk #2 FAILED at 690. Hunk #3
              FAILED at 707. Hunk #4 succeeded at 664 (offset -52 lines).
              Hunk #5 succeeded at 1196 (offset -67 lines). 3 out of 5
              hunks FAILED", exit 1 -- 7.x has neither
              exfat_dir_readahead() nor the 128 KiB EXFAT_MAX_RA_SIZE
              constant to bound. NOTE THE SHAPE, it is worse than 0050's:
              two hunks would SUCCEED there (the public wrapper, whose
              context survives the 7.x refactor, and the
              exfat_count_dir_entries() call site), so a forced apply
              would leave exfat_get_dentry() calling a nonexistent
              exfat_get_dentry_ra() and the public wrapper recursing into
              itself -- a compile error at best. Buildroot's -F0 applier
              exits 1 and stops the build first, which is the fail-closed
              behaviour this exclusion relies on. Retires together with
              0050 the moment the 6.18.y pin leaves 6.18.y.
7.x IS NOT
FIXED:        Read this before assuming the 7.x exclusion means 7.x is
              fine. It is not the 0047 situation (7.x already has the
              same commit) nor quite the 0050 situation (7.x has
              mainline's own equivalent). 7.2.6's exfat_get_dentry()
              reads ahead `ra_count = sect_per_clus`, capped by
              EXFAT_BLK_RA_SIZE(sb) = min(bdi->ra_pages, bdi->io_pages)
              -- 128 KiB at the default read_ahead_kb (7.2.6
              fs/exfat/exfat_fs.h:84-86, fatent.c:159-185, dir.c:645-658)
              -- and exfat_count_dir_entries() (7.2.6 dir.c:1175-1215)
              still goes through that same unbounded-for-its-purpose
              path. So the SAME read amplification exists on 7.x in a
              different shape. A 7.x fix would be ORIGINAL code, not a
              re-anchor of this hunk, and therefore has no fork commit to
              trace to; it is recorded as an open item in
              docs/kernel-recon/fork-sync-2026-09-21.md rather than
              invented here.
Forward-port: None needed on 6.18 -- applied as authored, added lines
              byte-identical to the fork commit's.

Signed-off-by: Nigel Breslaw <nigel.breslaw@slint.dev>
